    arrow small_a from.x 180 from.y 318 to.x 118 to.y 378 hidden true

    card title "How many Earths fit to the Moon?"

Here is the Earth, and here is the Moon, drawn to scale. How many Earths could you line up in the gap between them?
    draw earth earth_l moon moon_l

    draw gap_line dist n

    ask "How many Earths, side by side, would fill the gap to the Moon? Click on the line." on n store g default "10" tolerance 0.06
        right: Close. You have a good eye for distance.
        wrong: Not quite. Watch the Earths line up.

    hide n

About thirty. The Moon is much farther away than most pictures suggest.
    draw earths t30

    erase t30 dist gap_line earths

But here's the real surprise. Start lining up the other planets: Mercury, Venus, Mars.
    draw small small_l small_a

    erase small_l small_a

Then the giants: Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.
    draw giants

All seven fit in the gap. Just.
    draw spare

It's tight, though. Jupiter and Saturn spin so fast they bulge at the middle, so they have to stand pole to pole, like here.
    highlight jupiter saturn

And when the Moon is at its farthest, they fit whichever way you turn them.
    move moon by [44,0] duration 1.5

So more than half of that gap is just Jupiter and Saturn.
    highlight jupiter saturn
---
